\begin_layout Title
Chapter 3 - Consumer Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Check: Problem 3, Section 3.5.7
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3.5
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consumer Theory IV: Revealed Preference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now turn to the study of the consumer's decision problem.
 What are the testable implications of consumers' preference maximization?
 For simplicity, we assume throughout this section that prices are strictly
 positive.
 We denote the set of Walrasian budget sets with strictly positive prices
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{++}^{W}$
\end_inset

.
 We assume throughout that 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.5.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Preference maximization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Maximization of preferences implies WARP and it is equivalent to SARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Recall from our abstract study of choice that the choices of the agent
 represented by 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 in a budget set 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)\in\mathcal{B}^{W}$
\end_inset

 are given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C^{\succsim}(p,w)\equiv\left\{ x\in B(p,w):\forall\ y\in B(p,w),\ x\succsim y\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.5.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Utility maximization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that preference 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is represented by utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 if and only if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)\geq u(y)$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is chosen by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 if and only if it solves the 
\series bold
Utility Maximization Problem (UMP):
\series default

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\max\ u(x)\\
\mbox{subject to}\ x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\ \mbox{and}\ p\cdot x\leq w
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Thus, the consumer's problem of choosing her most preferred consumption
 bundle given prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and wealth level 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

 can now be stated as a UMP.
 In the UMP, the consumer chooses a consumption bundle in the Walrasian
 budget set 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 to maximize her utility level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(75).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 be a set and 
\begin_inset Formula $f:A\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 a real valued function defined on 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 An element 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in A$
\end_inset

 is a 
\series bold
maximizer of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset


\series default
 if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in A$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)\geq f(y)$
\end_inset

.
 The set of maximizers of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\arg\max_{x\in A}f(x)$
\end_inset

, or alternatively 
\begin_inset Formula $\arg\max\{f(x):x\in A\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Using the notation above, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C^{\succsim}(p,w)=\arg\max\left\{ u(x):x\in B(p,w)\right\} =\{x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}|\forall\ y\in B(p,w):x\succsim y\}
\]

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is the choice set induced by the maximization of preferences.
 So, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 solves the UMP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.5.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Characterization of demand correspondence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(17).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a continuous preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
By definition, the budget set 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)=\{x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:p\cdot x\leq\}$
\end_inset

 is closed.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is also bounded because for any 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}\leq(\nicefrac{w}{p_{l}})$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 So, by the Heine-Borel Theorem, each set 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is compact.
 By Theorem 9, a continuous preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 admits a continuous utility representation.
 By Weierstrass's Theorem, each continuous function on a compact set has
 a maximizer.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)\neq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With this result, we now focus our attention on the properties of two objects
 that emerge from the UMP: the consumer's set of optimal consumption bundles
 (the solution set of the UMP) and the consumer's maximal utility value
 (the value function of the UMP).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The rule that assigns the set of optimal consumption vectors in the UMP
 to each price-wealth situation 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\gg0$
\end_inset

 is represented by the choice structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C^{\succsim})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 is known as the 
\emph on
Walrasian
\emph default
 (or 
\emph on
ordinary
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
market
\emph default
) 
\emph on
demand correspondence
\emph default
.
 Note that, as a general matter, for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\gg0$
\end_inset

, the optimal set 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 may have more than one element.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 is single-valued for all 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

, we refer to 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, which is the unique element in 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, as the 
\emph on
Walrasian 
\emph default
(or 
\emph on
ordinary
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
market
\emph default
) 
\emph on
demand function
\emph default
.
 Remember, the choices made my an agent represented by a preference relation,
 i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, satisfy WARP by proposition 5.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The properties of 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 stated in Proposition 18 follow from direct examination of the UMP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(18).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a rational, locally non-satiated, and continuous preference and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mathcal{B}_{++}^{W},C^{\succsim}\right)$
\end_inset

 its associated Walrasian choice structure.
 Then,
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero, i.e., for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mathcal{B}_{++}^{W},C^{\succsim}\right)$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' Law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is convex, then 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 is convex-valued, i.e., for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is convex.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is strictly convex, then 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 is single-valued.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a rational, locally non-satiated, and continuous preference and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mathcal{B}_{++}^{W},C^{\succsim}\right)$
\end_inset

 its associated Walrasian choice structure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C^{\succsim}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=\left\{ x\in B(\alpha p,\alpha w):\forall\ y\in B(\alpha p,\alpha w),\ x\succsim y\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $B(\alpha p,\alpha w)\equiv\{x\in X:\alpha p\cdot x\leq\alpha w\}$
\end_inset

.
 If we divide both sides of the inequality by 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

, then the budget set is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $B(\alpha p,\alpha w)=B(p,w)\equiv\left\{ x\in X:p\cdot x\leq w\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 The set of feasible consumption bundles in the utility maximization problem
 does not change when all prices and wealth are multiplied by a constant
 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, we can rewrite 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(\alpha p,\alpha w)$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
C^{\succsim}(\alpha p,\alpha w) & = & \left\{ x\in B(p,w):\forall\ y\in B(p,w),\ x\succsim y\right\} \\
 & = & C^{\succsim}(p,w)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ (p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{++}:C^{\succsim}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Second, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mathcal{B}_{++}^{W},C^{\succsim}\right)$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' Law.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mathcal{B}_{++}^{W},C^{\succsim}\right)$
\end_inset

 does not satisfy Walras' Law.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ (p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \exists\ x\in C^{\succsim}(p,w):p\cdot x\neq w$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)\subseteq B(p,w)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x<w$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mbox{Int}(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

, which, by definition, is an open set.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Int}(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

 is an open set, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \epsilon>0:B_{\epsilon}^{X}(x)\subseteq\mbox{Int}(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is locally non-satiated, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ y\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(x):y\succ x$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(x)\subseteq\mbox{Int}(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\mbox{Int}(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\mbox{Int}(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mathcal{B}_{++}^{W},C^{\succsim}\right)$
\end_inset

 Walras' Law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we prove that if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is convex, then 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 is convex-valued.
 Assume preferences are convex.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is convex, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq C^{\succsim}(p,w),\ \forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:\alpha x+(1-\alpha)x^{\prime}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\cdot(\alpha x+(1-\alpha)x^{\prime})=\alpha\left(p\cdot x\right)+(1-\alpha)\left(p\cdot x^{\prime}\right)\leq\alpha w+(1-\alpha)w=w
\]

\end_inset

Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x+(1-\alpha)x^{\prime}\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, which is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Let us consider 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in U(\succsim,x^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is complete, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\succsim x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\in U(\succsim,x^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is convex, then 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succsim,x^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is a convex set.
 By convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succsim,x^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq U(\succsim,x^{\prime})\ \wedge\ \alpha\in[0,1]\implies\alpha x+(1-\alpha)x^{\prime}\in U(\succsim,x^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 When considering 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\succsim x$
\end_inset

, we also obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x+(1-\alpha)x^{\prime}\in U(\succsim,x)$
\end_inset

 by symmetric arguments.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x+(1-\alpha)x^{\prime}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is convex.
 Therefore, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is convex, then for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is convex, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is convex-valued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, we prove that if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is stricly-convex, then 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 is single-valued.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is strictly convex.
 Suppose for contradiction that
