\begin_layout Subsection*
3.5.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Continuity of choice
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The following result is a direct consequence of a mathematical result known
 as the Theorem of the Maximum (or Maximum Theorem).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(19).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a continuous preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 is single-valued.
 Then, the demand function associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\mathcal{B}_{++}^{W},C^{\succsim}\right)$
\end_inset

 is continuous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The result is a direct consequence of a mathematical result known as the
 Theorem of the Maximum (or Maximum Theorem).
 For details see Section 3.4.5 of the notes and Sundaram (1996).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Mathematical aside: The Theorem of the Maximum
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For details and proofs of this section see Sndaram (1996).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.5.5
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Constrained maximization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The theorem of Kuhn-Tucker provides conditions that allow us to identify
 points of maxima.
 You can find a complete treatment of the topic and a more general version
 of the theorem in Sundaram (1996).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is continuously differentiable, an optimal consumption bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 can be characterized in a very useful manner by means of first-order conditions.
 The 
\emph on
Kuhn-Tucker 
\emph default
(
\emph on
necessary
\emph default
) 
\emph on
conditions
\emph default
 say that if 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is a solution to the UMP, then there exists a 
\emph on
Lagrage multiplier
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\geq0$
\end_inset

 such that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
To be fully rigorous, these Kuhn-Tucker necessary conditions are valid only
 if the constraint qualificaiton condition holds.
 In the UMP, this is always so.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial u(x^{*})}{\partial x_{l}}\leq\lambda p_{l},\ \mbox{with equality if \ensuremath{x_{l}^{*}>0}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Equivalently, if we let 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u(x)=\left[\nicefrac{\partial u(x)}{\partial x_{l}},\ldots,\nicefrac{\partial u(x)}{\partial x_{L}}\right]$
\end_inset

 denote the gradient vector of 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, we can write (5) in matrix notation as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\nabla u(x^{*})\leq\lambda p
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x^{*}\cdot\left[\nabla u(x^{*})-\lambda p\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus, if we are at an interior optimum (i.e., if 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\gg0$
\end_inset

), we must have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\nabla u(x^{*})=\lambda p
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Condition (8) tells us that at an interior optimum, the gradient vector
 of the consumer's utility funciton 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u(x^{*})$
\end_inset

 must be proportional to the price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u(x^{*})\gg0$
\end_inset

, this is equivalent to the requirement that for any two goods 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\nicefrac{\partial u(x^{*})}{\partial x_{l}}}{\nicefrac{\partial u(x^{*})}{\partial x_{k}}}=\frac{p_{l}}{p_{k}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The expression on the left of (9) is the 
\emph on
marginal rate of substitution of good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 for good 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset


\emph default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{lk}(x^{*})$
\end_inset

; it tells us the amount of good 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 that the consumer must be given to compensate her for a one-unit reduction
 in her consumption of good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that if utility is unchanged with differential changes in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{k}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $dx_{l}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $dx_{k}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $[\nicefrac{\partial u(x)}{\partial x_{l}}]dx_{l}+[\nicefrac{\partial u(x)}{\partial x_{k}}]dx_{k}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}$
\end_inset

 falls by amount 
\begin_inset Formula $dx_{l}<0$
\end_inset

, the increase required in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{k}$
\end_inset

 to keep utility unchanged is precisely 
\begin_inset Formula $dx_{k}=MRS_{lk}(x^{*})(-dx_{l})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

Were this note the case, the consumer could do better by marginally changing
 her consumption.
 For example, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{[\nicefrac{\partial u(x^{*})}{\partial x_{l}}]}{[\nicefrac{\partial u(x^{*})}{\partial x_{k}}]}>(\nicefrac{p_{l}}{p_{k}})$
\end_inset

, then an increase in the consumption of good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $dx_{l}$
\end_inset

, would be feasible and would yield a utility change of 
\begin_inset Formula $[\nicefrac{\partial u(x^{*})}{\partial x_{l}}]dx_{l}-[\nicefrac{\partial u(x^{*})}{\partial x_{k}}](\nicefrac{p_{l}}{p_{k}})dx_{l}>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the case when the consumer's optimal bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 lies on the boundary of the consumption set, the gradient vector need not
 be proportional to the price vector.
 In particular, the first-order conditions tell us that 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial u(x^{*})}{\partial x_{l}}\leq\lambda p_{l}$
\end_inset

 for those 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}^{*}=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial u(x^{*})}{\partial x_{l}}=\lambda p_{l}$
\end_inset

 for those 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}^{*}>0$
\end_inset

.
 In contrast with the case of an interior optimum, an inequality between
 the marginal rate of substitution and the price ratio can arise at a boundary
 optimum because the consumer is unable to reduce her consumption of a good
 any further.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Lagrange multiplier 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 in the first-order conditions (6) and (7) gives the marginal, or shadow,
 value of relaxing the constraint in the UMP (this is a general property
 of Lagrange multipliers).
 It therefore equals the consumer's marginal utility of wealth at the optimum.
 To see this directly, consider for simplicity the case where 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is a differentiable function and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\gg0$
\end_inset

.
 By the chain rule, the change in utility from a marginal increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u(x(p,w))\cdot D_{w}x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Substituting for 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u(x(p,w))$
\end_inset

 from condition (8), we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\nabla u(x(p,w))\cdot D_{w}x(p,w)=\lambda p\cdot D_{w}x(p,w)=\lambda
\]

\end_inset

where the last equality follows because 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

 holds for all 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 (Walras' law) and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot D_{w}x(p,w)=1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the marginal change in utility arising from a marginal increase in
 wealth, the consumer's 
\emph on
marginal utility of wealth
\emph default
, is precisely 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that if monotonicity of 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is strengthened slightly by requiring that 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u(x)\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u(x)\neq0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, then condition (8) and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

also imply that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is strictly positive at any solution of the UMP.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have seen that conditions (6) and (7) must necessarily be satisfied by
 any 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 When, on the other hand, does satisfaction of these first-order conditions
 by some bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 imply that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a solution to the UMP? That is, when are the first-order conditions
 
\emph on
sufficient
\emph default
 to establish 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a solution? If 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is quasi-concave and monotone and has 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u(x)\neq0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 , then the Kuhn-Tucker first-order conditions are indeed sufficient.
 What if 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is not quasiconcave? In that case, if 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is locally quasiconcave at 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

, and if 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the first-order conditions, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a local maximum.
 Local quasiconcavity can be verified by means of a determinant test on
 the 
\emph on
borded Hessian matrix
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.5.6
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Indirect utility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(80).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a preference relation represented by a utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
indirect utility function associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset


\series default
 is the function, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}$
\end_inset

, that associated with each price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and each wealth level 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 the maximum value of utility, with respect to welfare index 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, attained at choice problem 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 That is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
V^{u}: & \mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & (p,w) & \mapsto & V^{u}(p,w)\equiv\max_{x\in B(p,w)}u(x)
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(V^{u}(p,w)=u(x(p,w))$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The following propositions states properties of indirect utility functions
 (continuity follows from the Theorem of the Maximum).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(20).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 that is represented by a continuous utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}$
\end_inset

 is:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Homogeneous of degree zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Strictly increasing in wealth and non-increasing in each price component.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Quasi-convex, i.e., for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{u}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}:V^{u}(p,w)\leq\bar{u}\right\} $
\end_inset

 is convex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Continuous.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 that is represented by a continuous utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 Important to note, 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}$
\end_inset

 is defined as follows
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
V^{u}: & \mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & (p,w) & \mapsto & V^{u}(p,w)\equiv{\displaystyle \max_{x\in B(p,w)}}\ u(x)=u(x(p,w))
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

and since the sufficient conditions are satisfied, then the results of Propositi
on 19 hold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero.
 By Proposition 19, we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ (p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{++}:u(x(\alpha p,\alpha w))=u(x(p,w))$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Second, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing in wealth.
 So, holding 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 constant, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{w,w^{\prime}\}:w>w^{\prime}\implies V^{u}(\bar{p},w)>V^{u}(\bar{p},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{w,w^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}:w>w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and hold prices constant at 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{p}\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(\bar{p},w)>V^{u}(\bar{p},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $w>w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, then 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $B(\bar{p},w^{\prime})\subsetneq\mbox{Int}(B(\bar{p},w))$
\end_inset

.

\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
 Also, since Walras' Law is satisfied, then 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(\bar{p},w)\subseteq\mbox{Bd}(B(\bar{p},w))$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $B(\bar{p},w^{\prime})\cap C^{\succsim}(p,w)=\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in C^{\succsim}(\bar{p},w),\ \forall\ y\in B(\bar{p},w^{\prime}):x\succ y$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ x\in C^{\succsim}(p,w),\ \exists\ y\in B(\bar{p},w^{\prime}):y\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\mbox{Int}(B(\bar{p},w))$
\end_inset

, which is an open set by definition.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Int}(B(\bar{p},w))$
\end_inset

 is an open set, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \epsilon>0:B_{\epsilon}^{X}(y)\subseteq\mbox{Int}(B(\bar{p},w))$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is locally non-satiatied, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ z\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(y):z\succ y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(y)\subseteq\mbox{Int}(B(\bar{p},w))$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in\mbox{Int}(B(\bar{p},w))$
\end_inset

.
 By transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $z\succ y\succsim x\implies z\succ x$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $z\succ x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z\in\mbox{Int}(B(\bar{p},w))$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, our claim is true.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in C^{\succsim}(\bar{p},w),\ \forall\ y\in B(\bar{p},w^{\prime}):u(x)>u(y)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, holding 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{p}$
\end_inset

 fixed, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{w,w^{\prime}\}:w>w^{\prime}\implies u(x(\bar{p},w))>u(x(\bar{p},w^{\prime}))$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing in wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}$
\end_inset

 is non-increasing in each price component.
 So, holding 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{-l}$
\end_inset

 constant, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ l\in\{1,\ldots,L\},\ \forall\ \left\{ p_{l},p_{l}^{\prime}\right\} \in\mathbb{R}_{++}:p_{l}>p_{l}^{\prime}\implies V^{u}(p_{l},p_{-l},w)\leq V^{u}(p_{l}^{\prime},p_{-l},w)$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $l\in\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

 and take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p,p^{\prime}\}\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}>p_{l}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and
\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ k\neq l:p_{k}=p_{k}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Also, hold wealth constant at 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{w}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,\bar{w})\leq V^{u}(p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gneq p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,\bar{w})\subset B(p^{\prime},\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x^{\prime}\in C^{\succsim}(p^{\prime},\bar{w}),\ \forall\ x\in C^{\succsim}(p,\bar{w}):x^{\prime}\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ x^{\prime}\in C^{\succsim}(p^{\prime},\bar{w}),\ \exists\ x\in C^{\succsim}(p,\bar{w}):x\succ x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C^{\succsim}(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}(p,\bar{w})\subset B(p,\bar{w})\subset B(p^{\prime},\bar{w})$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(p^{\prime},\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 We now have 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(p^{\prime},\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\in C^{\succsim}(p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, our claim is true.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x^{\prime}\in C^{\succsim}(p^{\prime},\bar{w}),\ \forall\ x\in C^{\succsim}(p,\bar{w}):u(x^{\prime})\geq u(x)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x(p^{\prime},\bar{w}))\geq u(x(p,\bar{w}))$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}$
\end_inset

 is non-increasing in each price component.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To see that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is quasiconvex, take 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{u}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w)\leq\bar{u}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq\bar{u}$
\end_inset

.
 For any 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

, consider the price-wealth pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime\prime},w^{\prime\prime})=(\alpha p+(1-\alpha)p^{\prime},\alpha w+(1-\alpha)w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 To establish quasiconvexity, we want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p^{\prime\prime},w^{\prime\prime})\leq\bar{u}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, we show that for any 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime\prime}\cdot x\leq w^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

, we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)\leq\bar{u}$
\end_inset

.
 Note, first, that if 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime\prime}\cdot x\leq w^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha p\cdot x+(1-\alpha)p^{\prime}\cdot x\leq\alpha w+(1-\alpha)w^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

Hence, either 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x\leq w$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot x\leq w^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 (or both).
 If the former inequality holds, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)\leq V^{u}(p,w)\leq\bar{u}$
\end_inset

, and we have established the result.
 If the latter holds, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)\leq V^{u}(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})\leq\bar{u}$
\end_inset

, and the same conclusion follows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
(This proof was found in the Microeconomic Theory I notes of Stanford University
, but it was not attempted in Mas-Colell (1995)).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is continuous.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{n}\rightarrow p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{n}\rightarrow w$
\end_inset

 be the sequences of prices and wealth.
 We must show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}V^{u}(p^{n},w^{n})=V^{u}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, which we doe by showing that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim\inf_{n}V^{u}(p^{n},w^{n})\geq V^{u}(p,w)\geq\lim\sup_{n}V^{u}(p^{n},p^{n})\geq\lim\inf_{n}V^{u}(p^{n},w^{n})$
\end_inset

.
 The last inequality is true by definition, so we focus our attention the
 the first two inequalities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For the first inequality, let 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w)=u(x)$
\end_inset

, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{n}=\nicefrac{w^{n}}{(p^{n}\cdot x)}$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{n}x\in B(p^{n},w^{n})$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p^{n},w^{n})\geq u(a^{n}\cdot x).$
\end_inset

 By Walras' Law, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x=w$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{n}a^{n}=\lim\nicefrac{w^{n}}{(p^{n}\cdot x)}=\nicefrac{w}{(p\cdot x)}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, using the continuity of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim\inf_{n}V^{u}(p^{n},w^{n})\geq\lim\in u(a^{n}x)=u(x)=V^{u}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, which implies the first inequality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For the second inequality, let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\in x(p^{n},w^{n})$
\end_inset

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p^{n},w^{n})=u(x^{n})$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $n^{k}$
\end_inset

 be a subsequence along which 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}u(x^{n^{k}})=\lim\sup_{n}V^{u}(p^{n},w^{n})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{n}\gg0$
\end_inset

, the union of the budget sets defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{n^{k}},w^{n^{k}})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is bounded above by the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 whose 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

-th component is 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{i}=\nicefrac{(\sup w^{n})}{(\inf p_{i}^{n})}$
\end_inset

.
 Since the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x^{n^{k}}\}$
\end_inset

 is bounded, it has some accumulation point 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{n^{k}}\cdot x^{n^{k}}\leq w^{n^{k}}$
\end_inset

, it follows by taking limits that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x\leq w$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w)\geq u(x)=\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}u(x^{n^{k}})=\lim\sup_{n}V^{u}(p^{n},w^{n})$
\end_inset

, which implies the second inequality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma*

\series bold
\shape up
(3).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 represented by a continuously differentibale utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\succsim}$
\end_inset

 is single-valued and let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be the associated demand function.
 Suppose that for some 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\gg0$
\end_inset

, that the K-T F.O.C.
 for the associated UMP are satisfed at 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

, and that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is differentiable at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial w}(p,w)=\lambda$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is the multiplier associated with the budget constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x-w\leq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
We assume all of the sufficient conditions given in the Lemma.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w)=u(x(p,w))$
\end_inset

.
 Using the chain rule,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial V^{u}}{\partial w}(p,w)=\frac{\partial u(x(p,w))}{\partial x_{1}}\frac{\partial x_{1}(p,w)}{\partial w}+\cdots+\frac{\partial u(x(p,w))}{\partial x_{L}}\frac{\partial x_{L}(p,w)}{\partial w}
\]

\end_inset

In matrix notation, this is written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial w} & = & \nabla u(x(p,w))^{T}D_{w}x(p,w)\\
 & = & Du(x(p,w))D_{w}x(p,w)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $Du(x(p,w))=\nabla u(x(p,w))^{T}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $Du(x(p,w))$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times L$
\end_inset

 matrix and 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{w}x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 column vector.
 From K-T F.O.C., 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u(x(p,w))=\lambda p$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $Du(x(p,w))=\lambda p^{T}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial w}=\lambda p^{T}D_{w}x(p,w)
\]

\end_inset

By Walras' Law, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)=p^{T}x(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

.
 Differentiating with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, we get 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{T}D_{w}x(p,w)=1$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial w}=\lambda
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
The marginal, or shadow value, of relaxing the constraint in the UMP is
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

, which is known as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

shadow price.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 It equals the consumer's marginal utility value of ealth at the optimum.
\end_layout

