\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We wish to find the value of the Slutsky matrix associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 at some arbitrary 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S(p,w)=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 0\\
0 & 0
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Take 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and take 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Without loss of generality, let prices change from 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 In addition, let us compensate the wealth of the agent as prices change.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be the demand function associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 To simplify notation, define 
\begin_inset Formula $x\equiv x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\equiv x(p^{\prime},w^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{\prime}\equiv p^{\prime}\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 By Walras' law,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}^{\prime}x_{1}^{\prime}+p_{2}^{\prime}x_{2}^{\prime}=w^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

Also, since we are compensating wealth, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}^{\prime}x_{1}+p_{2}^{\prime}x_{2}=w^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

Hence, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}^{\prime}x_{1}^{\prime}+p_{2}^{\prime}x_{2}^{\prime}=p_{1}^{\prime}x_{1}+p_{2}^{\prime}x_{2}
\]

\end_inset

From the utility function, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=x_{2}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}^{\prime}=(x_{2}^{\prime})^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, the equation above becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
p_{1}^{\prime}(x_{2}^{\prime})^{2}+p_{2}^{\prime}x_{2}^{\prime} & = & p_{1}^{\prime}x_{2}^{2}+p_{2}^{\prime}x_{2}\nonumber \\
p_{1}^{\prime}\left[(x_{2}^{\prime})^{2}-x_{2}^{2}\right] & = & -p_{2}(x_{2}^{\prime}-x_{2})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}^{\prime}=x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiciton 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}^{\prime}\neq x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[(x_{2}^{\prime})^{2}-x_{2}^{2}\right]=(x_{2}^{\prime}+x_{2})(x_{2}^{\prime}-x_{2})
\]

\end_inset

and plugging this expression into equation (6) yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}^{\prime}(x_{2}^{\prime}+x_{2})=-p_{2}^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset

However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, we can conclude that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}^{\prime}=x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S(p,w)=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 0\\
0 & 0
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

